#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "945f684f-9707-5a0a-b5d7-0753f11b4385" :question/math-academy-id "e-5266" :question/problem "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2$." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 0 &= 4x^{2} + 8(y + 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - 0 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1/4} + ((y + 2)^{2})/(1/8) \\\\\nz - 0 &= ((x - 0)^{2})/(1/4) + ((y - (-2))^{2})/(1/8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0)$. Also, from the equation, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{x = 0; y =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "7e876c7b-849f-59ce-a654-881ec9464475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5269" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2$." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + 1 &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - (-1) &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124283 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/id #uuid "5ce5b329-ac5d-5581-b396-eadd569b9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-6087" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1$\nand the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\n\\frac{2^{2}}{4} - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\n1 - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\nz &= -\\frac{1}{3}(y + 5)^{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91629b47-a169-5a84-b346-3a6060d427cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x =-1; y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123827} {:answer/id #uuid "eb08632d-13fc-573b-bfab-c140c22df9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 2; z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123828} {:answer/id #uuid "0d180240-4603-530c-ab5d-91e22c827fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x =-1; z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123829} {:answer/id #uuid "3e124fab-0af2-5732-8b6e-0584d5c10112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x = 1; y =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123830} {:answer/id #uuid "76337fc6-9453-5cd5-a73c-79bfd0e0fde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x = 1; y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91629b47-a169-5a84-b346-3a6060d427cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x =-1; y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123827} :answer-field/id #uuid "e08caaef-c397-5aa6-9fa9-db3cd4c262e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123826}] :question/id #uuid "ef1d67f8-50b7-560a-9d0b-39e4e445375c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73856" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z = ((x + 1)^{2})/(2) + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4)$. Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"}]}
